            <text x="130" y="26" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-display), sans-serif" font-size="13.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-text-1)">dwarfs-t · dwarfs-t-rs</text>
            <text x="130" y="44" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">the image format — the only C++ in the</text>
            <text x="130" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">ecosystem — + its standalone Rust binding</text>
            <text x="130" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)">SHIPPED</text>
          </g>
          <g transform="translate(340, 440)">
            <rect width="260" height="92" rx="10" fill="var(--tb-c-card)" stroke="var(--tb-c-divider-strong)" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <text x="130" y="26" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-display), sans-serif" font-size="13.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-text-1)">the tebako CLI</text>
            <text x="130" y="44" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">the packager — press lean/fat, install,</text>
            <text x="130" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">publish, cache — one static binary</text>
            <text x="130" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)">tamatebako/tebako · SHIPPED</text>
          </g>
          <g transform="translate(650, 440)">
            <rect width="260" height="92" rx="10" fill="var(--tb-c-card)" stroke="var(--tb-c-divider-strong)" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <text x="130" y="26" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-display), sans-serif" font-size="13.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-text-1)">tebako-pkg · tfs</text>
            <text x="130" y="44" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">trailer surgery · the generic image CLI —</text>
            <text x="130" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--tb-c-text-3)">parity-pinned against the C++ oracle</text>
            <text x="130" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono), monospace" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)">SHIPPED in tamatebako/tebako</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        The gem-era orchestrator is retired: the C++/gem codebase is archived at{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.tebakoV1} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">tamatebako/tebako-v1</a>
        {' '}and the product is the Rust workspace at{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.tebako} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">tamatebako/tebako</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ THE THREE PARTS ============ -->
    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-3">The three parts of every package.</h2>
      <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        A tebako package is a composition of up to three independently distributable parts, glued by
        the <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">tpkg trailer</strong>: a 166-byte header at end
        of file plus 280-byte slot records. The format version is 1 forever — extensions ride flags
        (<code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">LEAN</code>,{' '}
        <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">SIGNED_V2</code>,{' '}
        <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">NO_INSTALL</code>), never version bumps, so v1
        readers parse v2 packages untouched. The full byte tables live in{' '}
        <a href="/docs/architecture/" class="tb-link-underline font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">concepts</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
        <img src="/assets/diagrams/package-anatomy.svg" alt="Anatomy of a tebako package: the bootstrap, an optional runtime image (fat packages), one to eight payload image slots, an optional v2 signature extension, and the tpkg trailer header at end of file" class="w-full min-w-[760px]" loading="lazy" />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <p class="max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
          <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">A · the bootstrap</strong> — the{' '}
          <a href={LINKS.bootstrapCrate} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">Rust loader</a>,
          a static binary under 3 MB. It is the process entry point of every stitched package: reads
          its own trailer, resolves the runtime, verifies, hands off.
        </p>
        <p class="max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
          <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">B · the runtime</strong> — two artifacts, not one:
          the interpreter executable and the <em>env image</em> (a{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">.tfs</code> carrying the runtime's files).
          They are downloaded once into the shared cache, sha256-verified, and{' '}
          <a href="/docs/architecture/runtime-as-image/" class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">mounted — never extracted</a>.
          {' '}Fat packages carry the runtime as a slot; lean packages carry a reference and fetch on
          first run.
        </p>
        <p class="max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
          <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">C · the payload slices</strong> — bare{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">.tfs</code> images, self-describing via an
          in-image manifest (<code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">/__tpkg__/manifest.yaml</code>).
          The container's <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">format_id</code> answers exactly
          one question — how to read the bytes (limnifs, dwarfs-t, squashfs, zip); whether a slot is a
          runtime and which payload carries the entrypoint are manifest semantics, never encoded in
          the format axis. LimniFS is the default image format
          {' '}since tebako v0.2.0; dwarfs-t stays first-class as a read backend.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ TWO WAYS + DIAGRAM 2 ============ -->
    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-3">Two ways to run it.</h2>
      <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        Same engine, two relationships between the developer and the user: hand over one file and
        disappear, or publish versioned payloads that a dispatcher assembles per invocation.
      </p>

      <!-- DIAGRAM 2: standalone vs managed -->
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 940 470" class="w-full min-w-[760px]" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Standalone mode versus managed mode, side by side">
          <defs>
            <marker id="sm-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="var(--tb-c-accent)" />
            </marker>
            <marker id="sm-arrow-gold" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="var(--color-gold)" />
            </marker>
          </defs>

