            <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-xl font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">1 · Standalone — ship the app, hide the machinery</h3>
            <StatusPill status="shipped" />
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            A developer presses their app once per platform with the{' '}
            <a href={LINKS.cliCrate} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">tebako CLI</a>
            {' '}— a single static binary, no host Ruby required — and publishes plain executables.
            Their users download one file and run it: no Ruby, no gem environment, no tebako to
            install, nothing to know. The <em>lean</em> variant keeps the download small: the first
            run fetches the shared runtime once into the machine-wide cache, where every tebako
            package on the machine reuses it. The <em>fat</em> variant carries the runtime inside
            and never touches the network.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg);">
            <img src="/assets/diagrams/lean-vs-fat.svg" alt="Lean versus fat package flows: lean resolves the runtime from the shared machine cache and downloads it once on a cache miss; fat embeds the runtime and never touches the network; both land in the same machine-wide cache" class="w-full min-w-[760px]" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
            <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">TEBAKO_OFFLINE=1</code> forces cache-only mode: a
            hit, or the named error <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">EX_TEBAKO_UNAVAILABLE</code>
            {' '}(exit 69) — never a surprise download.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-8" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
