    <!-- ============ THE ENGINE ============ -->
    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-3">The engine underneath.</h2>
      <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        TFS is a product in its own right: a userland virtual filesystem — mount table,
        longest-prefix dispatch, pluggable read-only backends, copy-on-write and encryption
        transforms above them. The{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.tfsCrate} class="tb-link-underline font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">Rust tfs crate</a>
        {' '}<strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">is</strong> the shipping implementation of the{' '}
        <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">tebako_fs_*</code> C ABI — 33 exports — and
        drives every mount on this page. The C++{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.libtfsRepo} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">libtfs</a>
        {' '}survives as the legacy parity oracle: the Rust ABI runs its contract suite, byte-for-byte.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
        <img src="/assets/diagrams/tfs-router.svg" alt="The TFS router: calls enter through the tebako_fs_* C ABI; the Rust router dispatches by longest mount-point prefix onto read-only backends — limnifs, dwarfs-t, squashfs, zip, tar — with a copy-on-write overlay above; paths outside every mount pass through to the host filesystem" class="w-full min-w-[760px]" loading="lazy" />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        Any project, in any language, can attach images to its binaries through the same ABI, or
        drive images from the shell with the{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.tfsCli} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">tfs CLI</a>.{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.dwarfsT} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">dwarfs-t</a> — the
        only C++ in the ecosystem — and{' '}
        <a href={LINKS.dwarfsTRs} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">dwarfs-t-rs</a>,
        its standalone Rust binding, are consumable without knowing tebako exists. Every backend is
        read-only at runtime forever; writing exists only as the copy-on-write overlay plus a
        creation-time Writer. The full model:{' '}
        <a href="/docs/architecture/vfs/" class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">the VFS model</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ THE TRUST CHAIN ============ -->
    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-3">The trust chain.</h2>
      <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        One signature mechanism — OpenPGP via rnp-rs — from the publisher's key to the user's run.
        Signing is per-package opt-in; verification of anything signed is always strict. Verification
        happens at install, never per run; failures are named exits, never crashes. Details:{' '}
        <a href="/docs/architecture/chain-of-trust/" class="tb-link-underline font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">the chain of trust</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
        <img src="/assets/diagrams/trust-chain.svg" alt="The tebako chain of trust: a root key signs release keys, release keys sign the release manifest, the manifest carries per-artifact SHA256 digests; on the package side the tpkg v2 trailer carries per-slot SHA256 and an OpenPGP signature, verified at install with named failure exits" class="w-full min-w-[760px]" loading="lazy" />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--color-gold)] p-6" style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-gold) 6%, transparent);">
        <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--color-gold)]">Stated plainly: the rollout phase</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
          Today's shipped loader is built <strong class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">without</strong>
          {' '}OpenPGP verification — the unverified-first rollout. Signed packages run with a loud
          {' '}<code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">UNVERIFIED</code> warning plus an audit-journal
          entry while their per-slot sha256 is still enforced as integrity; unsigned packages run
          with the legacy warning. <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">TEBAKO_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1</code>
          {' '}fails closed with exit 71 — a strict-mode request is never silently downgraded. Full
          signature verification in the loader returns with the crypto-toolkit payload. The target
          model — embedded root fingerprint, TOFU key pins under{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">$TEBAKO_HOME/trust/</code>, exits{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">70</code> (sha256 mismatch),{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">71</code> (bad signature),{' '}
          <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)]">72</code> (untrusted or changed signer key) — is on{' '}
          <a href="/docs/architecture/chain-of-trust/" class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">the chain-of-trust page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ AUDIENCES ============ -->
    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-3">Who it's for.</h2>
      <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        The audience rule, stated once: prebuilt artifacts flow downward; compilation never flows
        outward. Only people building tebako itself ever compile tebako.
      </p>
      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="tb-card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">People running packages</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            No compilation machinery, no libraries, nothing to install — download, run, done. With a
            fat package, not even a network. With tebako installed, one shims directory on PATH puts
            every managed tool a command away, with version pinning that just works.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tb-card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">Developers shipping data</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            Data-only payloads need no compilation machinery:{' '}
            <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">tfs mkimage</code>, a manifest, a release.
            Fonts, datasets, tool assets — content-addressed, signed if you want it, mounted whole.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tb-card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">Developers shipping applications</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            You compile only your own code. Tebako itself arrives as binaries; the runtimes are
            prebuilt downloads. Press once per platform in a matrix CI job — no heroics per target —
            and publish to releases you already own.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tb-card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">People hacking on tebako</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            The only audience that needs the tebako toolchain: the factories, the product workspace,
